BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Decadence and Seventy Book Launch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260930T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260930T190000
UID:bm-6203920260930@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/2026_09_30.png?cb=1787771783
DESCRIPTION:[at Ben McNally Books]\nJoin us at Ben McNally Books\, in part
 nership with Biblioasis and Random House Canada\, for a double book launch
 \, featuring both Richard Kelly Kemmick and Ian Brown.\nAbout the Books:\n
 Decadence\n"The genre of memoir is bullshit (as I humbly posit). No one ac
 tually lives like they are moving along some grand Freytag’s triangle. R
 ather\, we live within isolated instances\, specific struggles and victori
 es\, which\, when compiled\, form a narrative."—Richard Kelly Kemick.\nI
 n\nDecadence\n\, Richard Kelly Kemick’s “accidental memoir of a sort\,
 ” the author ranges widely through his myriad preoccupations and obsessi
 ons—volleyball\, municipal landfills\, dogs\, high school plays\, Christ
 mas villages\, love—out of which the shape of a unique sensibility is re
 vealed. Reminiscent of the three Davids—Rakoff\, Sedaris\, and Wallace
 —these essays accrete into a portrait of a man trying to make sense of a
  world in which there are no goddamn rules\; and yet one in which every ac
 tion has sometimes profound consequences.\nA book of intelligence and care
  and kindness and humour and yearning and the occasional epiphany\, Decade
 nce gathers up from the odds and ends of living what makes a modern life
 —quiet and desperate as it may at times be—worth celebrating.\nOrder a
  copy of\nDecadence\nhere\nSeventy\nIan Brown\, award-winning author of Th
 e Boy in the Moon and the memoir Sixty\, now recounts his daily reckoning 
 with the milestone age of seventy. Fearlessly candid\, often funny and rel
 entlessly real\, Brown asks the terrifying questions we all have about gro
 wing old\, even when he doesn’t want to know the answers.\nAn acclaimed 
 journalist and chronicler of contemporary life\, seventy-year-old Ian Brow
 n is not going gently into his eighth decade. The outlook is more perilous
  than it was at age sixty\, but Brown is determined (however hopelessly) n
 ot to let time get the better of him.\nDuring the course of his seventy-fi
 rst year\, Brown writes incessantly\, travelling on assignment for the Glo
 be and Mail across the United States in the months leading to the shocking
  2024 election\; holidays with family and friends\, always aware he might 
 not see them again\; skis and rows and exercises like a madman while turni
 ng a wary eye on the live-forever movement\; takes up pickleball and talks
  books\; and in general tries (and frequently fails) to show some human co
 urage in the face of the march of his dwindling years.\nAlong the way he e
 ncounters the lists that have suddenly become essential to daily life (wha
 t to take when leaving the house\, what to pack for a trip\, what to pick 
 up at the drugstore)\; the indignities of medical tests and the shock of f
 inancing a retirement\; the terrors and pleasures of growing older with on
 e’s spouse of four decades\; the now-suspenseful drama of sex\; the thri
 lling spectacle of watching adult children inhabit new roles of familial r
 esponsibility\; and the shocking new lessons of life as an older person (a
 ccept compliments with grace\, remember to smile\, stand up straighter).\n
 As the percentage of the Canadian population over eighty-five will triple 
 in the next twenty-five years\, Brown contemplates his mortality and that 
 of his contemporaries for readers of all ages. Growing old—anything afte
 r seventy—is a grave challenge\, but also enormously liberating: if ther
 e is something you want to say or do\, now is your chance to say it or do 
 it. “I have to keep taking chances\,” Brown reckons. “I don’t want
  to give in too soon.”\nOrder a copy of\nSeventy\nhere
LOCATION:Ben McNally Books
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Ravenous Book Launch
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T190000
UID:bm-6431420261002@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/10_03_26.png?cb=1790269087
DESCRIPTION:[at Ben McNally Books]\nJoin us at Ben McNally Books\, in part
 nership with Fernwood Publishing\, to celebrate the launch of\nRavenous: A
  Novel\n\, by Sara Cooper.\nOrder a copy of\nRavenous\nhere.\nAbout the Bo
 ok\nBreigh is a hot elder millennial who is obsessed with the brands she i
 s ambassading for. Her policy is YES\, and her livestreams are viral. But 
 she’s pregnant now\, and her body’s changing. It's not that she's hung
 ry. She's definitely not depressed. She just sometimes eats boxes of makeu
 p before passing out.\nDaddy’s bought a brand-new home for Breigh and Ba
 by\, complete with a Greenguard Gold-certified\, twice-imported velvet inf
 ant chaise — but is he cheating on her with a hotter\, younger coworker?
  Breigh has to get Baby out before it ruins her. She has to create more an
 d better content than the other Momfluencers. She has to unbox something n
 ew. She has to unbox … Baby! Baby approves of her seven-step marketing p
 lan. Welcome to #LiveBirth.\nBreigh’s increasingly unhinged life reflect
 s her generation’s anxieties around psychological manipulation through s
 ocial media platforms. Multi-award-winning author Sara Cooper presents a w
 ild debut novel in this incisive satire of consumerism\, performative femi
 ninity\, and social media.\nAbout the Author\nSara Cooper is a novelist an
 d playwright-lyricist whose work blends dark humor\, psychological insight
 \, and sharp cultural critique. She is the author of Dead Center and Raven
 ous\, both of which were finalist for the Tucson Festival of Books Literar
 y Awards (2025 and 2026). Her theatre work has received awards and honours
  from the American Academy of Arts and Letters\, American Theatre Wing\, N
 ew York State Council on the Arts\, National Alliance for Musical Theatre\
 , and ASCAP. She holds an MFA from New York University’s Graduate Musica
 l Theatre Writing Program and currently teaches at Purchase College and NY
 U. Learn more at saracooper.me.
LOCATION:Ben McNally Books
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Rise After Silence Book Launch and Concert
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261004T200000
UID:bm-6431620261004@shop.benmcnallybooks.com
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://cdn1.bookmanager.com/i/7600763/Website2/
 Events/10_04_26.png?cb=1790269236
DESCRIPTION:Join us at\nHugh's Room\n(296 Broadview Ave.) to celebrate th
 e launch of\nRise After Silence: An ER Doctor and the Patients Who Healed 
 Her\n\, a new memoir by Anne Aspler.\nThe evening will feature Anne perfo
 rming excerpts from the book with the support of a full rock band\, as wel
 l as a silent auction in support of\nEnd Violence Everywhere.\nTickets are
  available through Eventbrite here.\nOrder a copy of\nRise After Silence\n
 here.\nAbout the Book\nAt 5 a.m. in the emergency department\, Dr. Anne As
 pler is the physician treating patients in crisis. She collects forensic e
 vidence from sexual assault survivors. She sets the shattered bones of dom
 estic abuse victims. But beneath the white coat\, she is wrestling with a 
 crisis of her own.\nAnne's father is a convicted serial rapist. Her mother
  is lost to severe schizophrenia\, hoarding\, and the streets. Raised amid
  religious fundamentalism\, foster care\, and poverty\, Anne ran in the op
 posite direction-eventually becoming a Harvard-trained doctor. To survive\
 , she severed ties with her father\, shed her faith\, and built a pristine
 \, controlled life.\nBut the ER is a mirror. When Anne treats women injure
 d by their partners\, or patients wrestling with PTSD and addiction\, the 
 boundary between doctor and patient collapses. Caring for them forces her 
 to ask an impossible question: How will she explain her parents to her you
 ng daughters-and should they ever meet their grandfather?\nRise After Sile
 nce\nis a visceral\, unsparing memoir about the collision of professional 
 duty and deeply buried personal trauma. It is an exploration of how privat
 e violence never stays private. It compounds. It migrates. And it demands 
 to be brought into the light. An unflinching look at what we inherit-and t
 he fierce work of deciding what we pass on.\nAbout the Author\nAnne Aspler
  is a Harvard-trained emergency physician and brain health speaker. Childh
 ood experiences of food insecurity and family violence drew her to medicin
 e — work that has taken her from her own emergency department to more th
 an thirty countries. Her debut memoir\, Rise After Silence: An ER Doctor a
 nd the Patients Who Healed Her\, weaves her patients' stories together wit
 h her own account of survival and recovery. She also writes Radical Brain 
 Health on Substack\, where she explores emotional resilience and cognitive
  longevity. She lives in Toronto with her husband and two daughters.\nAuth
 or website: anneaspler.com
LOCATION:Hugh's Room\, 296 Broadview Ave
URL:https://shop.benmcnallybooks.com/events
X-SOURCE:Ben McNally Books
END:VEVENT
